\b ## Section B: Long Scenario Questions (50 Points)\b0\par
\par
\b Question 33: Virtualization Infrastructure and Image Management (25 Points)\b0\par
\par
\b Scenario:\b0  You are a Junior Cloud Engineer at a startup. You have been tasked with setting up a development environment on a fresh Ubuntu server using KVM. The team needs a base "Golden Image" that they can quickly clone for new developer workstations.\par
\par
1.1. Before installing any software, you need to verify if the physical server's CPU (Intel-based) supports hardware virtualization. Provide the exact command to check for the required CPU flag and specify what that flag is called for Intel processors. [4 Points]\par
\par
1.2. The installation requires a 50GB virtual disk. Your manager wants the disk to be "Thin Provisioned" and support snapshots to save space. Which disk format should you use, and what is the exact qemu-img command to create this 50GB disk named base_dev.qcow2? [5 Points]\par
\par
1.3. You need to install the VM non-interactively via the CLI using an ISO located at /iso/ubuntu.iso. The VM should have 4GB of RAM and 2 vCPUs. Provide the virt-install command to accomplish this. [6 Points]\par
\par
1.4. Once the "Golden Image" is ready (VM named dev_base), a developer asks for a clone named workstation_1. To save storage, you must use a "Linked Clone" methodology.\par
a) Explain the main difference in storage behavior between a Full Clone and a Linked Clone. [3 Points]\par
b) Provide the VBoxManage command (assuming testing in a VirtualBox environment) or the virt-clone concept to create a clone. [2 Points]\par
\par
1.5. A week later, workstation_1 has become unstable. You want to revert it to a snapshot titled "Fresh_Install" that you took previously. Provide the virsh command to list all snapshots for the VM and the command to revert to that specific snapshot. [5 Points]\par
\par
\b Answer:\b0\par
1.1. \f0\fs22 grep -E 'vmx' /proc/cpuinfo\f1\fs24 . Flag is \b vmx\b0 .\par
1.2. Format: \b qcow2\b0 . Command: \f0\fs22 qemu-img create -f qcow2 base_dev.qcow2 50G\f1\fs24\par
1.3. \f0\fs22 virt-install --name dev_base --memory 4096 --vcpus 2 --disk path=base_dev.qcow2 --cdrom /iso/ubuntu.iso --os-variant ubuntu22.04\f1\fs24\par
1.4. a) Full Clone is a complete copy (full space); Linked Clone is a 'delta' copy sharing a read-only base image. b) \f0\fs22 VBoxManage clonevm "dev_base" --name "workstation_1" --register --options link\f1\fs24\par
1.5. List: \f0\fs22 virsh snapshot-list workstation_1\f1\fs24 . Revert: \f0\fs22 virsh snapshot-revert workstation_1 Fresh_Install\f1\fs24\par
\par
\b Question 34: Enterprise Storage and Persistence (25 Points)\b0\par
\par
\b Scenario:\b0  Your company's database server is running out of space. You have attached two new 50GB disks (/dev/sdb and /dev/sdc) and need to configure them using LVM for flexibility.\par
\par
1.1. Initialize both disks as Physical Volumes (PVs) and create a new Volume Group (VG) named production-vg using both disks. [5 Points]\par
\par
1.2. Create a 60GB Logical Volume (LV) named db-data inside the production-vg. Format it with the ext4 filesystem. [6 Points]\par
\par
1.3. You want this volume to mount automatically to /mnt/database every time the system boots.\par
a) Why is using the UUID in /etc/fstab better than using the device path like /dev/sdb1? [3 Points]\par
b) Provide the command to find the UUID of your new Logical Volume. [2 Points]\par
\par
1.4. The team decides they need even more space. You add a third disk and extend the VG.\par
a) Provide the command to extend the Logical Volume db-data by an additional 20GB. [2 Points]\par
b) What is the "Online Resize" command required so the Linux OS recognizes the new space without unmounting? [2 Points]\par
\par
1.5. RAID vs ZFS:\par
a) Explain why RAID 0 is considered dangerous for production data. [2 Points]\par
b) ZFS handles both volume management and file systems. Provide the command to check the health/status of a ZFS pool named datapool. [3 Points]\par
\par
\b Answer:\b0\par
1.1. \f0\fs22 sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb /dev/sdc\f1\fs24  followed by \f0\fs22 sudo vgcreate production-vg /dev/sdb /dev/sdc\f1\fs24\par
1.2. \f0\fs22 sudo lvcreate -n db-data -L 60G production-vg\f1\fs24 . Format: \f0\fs22 sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/production-vg/db-data\f1\fs24\par
1.3. a) UUID is persistent and doesn't change if disk ordering changes. b) \f0\fs22 blkid /dev/production-vg/db-data\f1\fs24\par
1.4. a) \f0\fs22 sudo lvextend -L +20G /dev/production-vg/db-data\f1\fs24 . b) \f0\fs22 sudo resize2fs /dev/production-vg/db-data\f1\fs24\par
1.5. a) RAID 0 has no redundancy; one disk failure loses all data. b) \f0\fs22 zpool status datapool\f1\fs24\par
}
